¶
'''tit'''<sup>3</sup> (tit), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. also ''titt;'' appar. orig. 'something small.' Cf. ''tit''<sup>2</sup>, ''titty''<sup>2</sup>. Cf. also ''tot''<sup>1</sup>.] 1. A small or poor horse.

{{smaller block|The nag or the hackeneie is verie good for trauelling. . . . And if he be broken accordinglie, you shall haue a little ''tit'' that will trauell a whole daie without anie bait.
{{right|''Stanihurst,'' Descrip. of Ireland, ii. (Holinshed's Chron., I.).}}}}

{{smaller block|The Modern Poets seem to use Smut as the Old Ones did Machines, to relieve a fainting Invention. When Pegasus is jaded, and would stand still, he is apt, like other ''Tits,'' to run into every Puddle.
{{right|''Jeremy Collier,'' Short View (ed. 1698), p. 6.}}}}

2. A child; a girl; a young woman: a depreciatory term.

{{smaller block|I wonder that any man is so mad to come to see these rascally ''tits'' play here.
{{right|''B. Jonson,'' Cynthia's Revels, Ind.}}}}
